  <graph edgedefault="undirected">
    <node id="&quot;JOHN SPARKE (DIED 1566)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"John Sparke (died 1566) was an English politician who served in the House of Commons and held various local positions in Plympton Erle."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0344-2e30be8d9090</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NANTWICH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nantwich is the original place of origin for John Sparke (died 1566)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nantwich is the original place of origin for John Sparke (died 1566)."(</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0344-2e30be8d9090</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLYMPTON ST. MAURICE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Plympton St. Maurice is the location where John Sparke (died 1566) settled and was buried."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plympton St. Maurice is the location where John Sparke (died 1566) settled and was buried."(</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0344-2e30be8d9090</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLYMPTON ERLE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Plympton Erle is the constituency for which John Sparke (died 1566) was elected as a Member of Parliament."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plympton Erle is the constituency for which John Sparke (died 1566) was elected as a Member of Parliament."(</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0344-2e30be8d9090</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLYMPTON ST MAURICE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Plympton St Maurice is the church where John Sparke (died 1566) was buried."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plympton St Maurice is the church where John Sparke (died 1566) was buried."(</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0344-2e30be8d9090</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;JANE MOORE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Jane Moore is the wife of John Sparke (died 1566) and mother of his four sons."&lt;SEP&gt;"Jane Moore is the wife of John Sparke (died 1566) and mother of his four sons."(</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0344-2e30be8d9090</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;JOHN SPARKE (DIED 1680)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"John Sparke (died 1680) was an English landowner and politician who served in the House of Commons from 1677 to 1680."&lt;SEP&gt;"John Sparke (died 1680) was an English landowner and politician who served in the House of Commons from 1677 to 1680."(</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0344-2e30be8d9090</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;JONATHAN SPARKE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Jonathan Sparke is the father of John Sparke (1636 - 8 October 1680) and the second son of John Sparke (c 1574–1640), MP, of The Friary, in the parish of St Jude, Plymouth."&lt;SEP&gt;"Jonathan Sparke is the father of John Sparke (1636 - 8 October 1680) and the second son of John Sparke (c 1574–1640), MP, of The Friary, in the parish of St Jude, Plymouth."(</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0344-2e30be8d9090</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MARY BASSET&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mary Basset is the mother of John Sparke (1636 - 8 October 1680) and the daughter of Sir Robert Basset of Heanton Punchardon."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mary Basset is the mother of John Sparke (1636 - 8 October 1680) and the daughter of Sir Robert Basset of Heanton Punchardon."(</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0344-2e30be8d9090</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;COSIMO III DE' MEDICI, GRAND DUKE OF TUSCANY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cosimo III de' Medici, Grand Duke of Tuscany visited Plymouth and mentioned Jonathan Sparke in his travel journal."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cosimo III de' Medici, Grand Duke of Tuscany visited Plymouth and mentioned Jonathan Sparke in his travel journal."(</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0344-2e30be8d9090</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THE FRIARY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The Friary is the residence of the Sparke family and the location of Jonathan Sparke."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Friary is the residence of the Sparke family and the location of Jonathan Sparke."(</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0344-2e30be8d9090</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ST JUDE, PLYMOUTH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"St Jude, Plymouth is the parish of The Friary, where the Sparke family resided."&lt;SEP&gt;"St Jude, Plymouth is the parish of The Friary, where the Sparke family resided."(</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0344-2e30be8d9090</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLYMOUTH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Plymouth is the city where the Sparke family resided and where the Friary is located."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plymouth is the city where the Sparke family resided and where the Friary is located."(</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0344-2e30be8d9090</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SPARKE FAMILY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The Sparke family owned and resided in the Friary in Plymouth."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Sparke family owned and resided in the Friary in Plymouth."(</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0344-2e30be8d9090</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MOLESWORTHS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The Molesworths acquired the property from the Sparke family."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Molesworths acquired the property from the Sparke family."(</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0344-2e30be8d9090</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CLARKES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The Clarkes acquired the property from the Molesworths."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Clarkes acquired the property from the Molesworths."(</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0344-2e30be8d9090</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BEWESES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The Beweses acquired the property from the Clarkes."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Beweses acquired the property from the Clarkes."(</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0344-2e30be8d9090</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HOSPITAL FOR SOLDIERS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The buildings were converted into a hospital for soldiers in 1794."&lt;SEP&gt;"The buildings were converted into a hospital for soldiers in 1794."(</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0344-2e30be8d9090</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;INFIRMARY FOR THE TROOPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Parts of the buildings were used as an infirmary for troops stationed at Millbay and Frankfort Barracks."&lt;SEP&gt;"Parts of the buildings were used as an infirmary for troops stationed at Millbay and Frankfort Barracks."(</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0344-2e30be8d9090</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FRIARY COURT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Friary Court was the area where the buildings were located and was no longer considered a fashionable address by 1890."&lt;SEP&gt;"Friary Court was the area where the buildings were located and was no longer considered a fashionable address by 1890."(</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0344-2e30be8d9090</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WEST INDIA EXPEDITION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The West India expedition was the reason for the conversion of the buildings into a hospital for soldiers in 1794."&lt;SEP&gt;"The West India expedition was the reason for the conversion of the buildings into a hospital for soldiers in 1794."(</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0344-2e30be8d9090</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DEADLY SICKNESS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A deadly sickness was the cause of the conversion of the buildings into a hospital for soldiers in 1794."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0344-2e30be8d9090</data>
    </node>
  </graph>
